--- a/docs/reference/03-orchestrator.md
+++ b/docs/reference/03-orchestrator.md
@@ -31,6 +31,37 @@ This chapter documents the workflow behaviour from the conductor's side — entr
 
 ---
 
+## Completion observation boundary
+
+The executable and completion hooks read one command grammar in
+`core/tools/aidlc-orchestrator-request.ts`. It owns the existing launcher and
+report options, duplicate-option behavior, literal result values, and report
+branch selection. `aidlc.ts` still owns the outer dispatcher route. The shell
+adapter in `aidlc-report-command.ts` captures supported literal invocations and
+passes their argv, directory and environment to those same owners; it does not
+interpret feedback text as options or invent intent/space report flags.
+
+`aidlc-report-target.ts` resolves the decoded request through record-core with
+binding disabled. It returns the selected project, session, record, stage and
+attempt facts. Isolated attempts read only their own start and completion
+history, including valid space-only attempts; they never borrow a main
+workflow's state or review. Project precedence and audit attempt boundaries
+have independent helpers, shared with runtime readers.
+
+These core modules have no Rin dependency or autonomy mode. A build omitting
+Rin's autonomy hook uses the same request, selection and report behavior. The
+constitution and review hooks consume the observation separately and retain
+their own evidence and refusal policies. The Rin review guard currently has no
+isolated-attempt authorization path, so a covered isolated attempt is refused
+explicitly rather than borrowing a main-workflow verdict.
+
+The observer supports native `aidlc`/`aidlc.exe`, copied dispatcher, and direct
+orchestrator commands. It is not a general shell evaluator. Known reports with
+unresolved dynamic context, session-changing prefixes or code-loading wrappers
+are refused with a diagnostic. A projection must deliver the command observer
+with the guard and the corresponding target/runtime modules; source tests or
+an explicitly assembled payload do not prove host hook registration.
+
 ## Entry Points
 
 The conductor passes `$ARGUMENTS` to the engine's first `next` verbatim — it never pre-parses them. The engine parses the flags and freeform text and resolves which of the invocation patterns below applies, emitting the matching directive. The patterns are engine-resolved inputs, not conductor-side branches.
